# Evidence Board

## DOCUMENTED

* Bagalāmukhī is established as one of the ten Mahāvidyās in relatively late Śākta-tantric systems and is strongly associated with *stambhana*: stopping, immobilizing or paralyzing an opponent’s action and speech. ([The Metropolitan Museum of Art][2])
* Her most common two-armed iconography shows her grasping an opponent’s tongue while wielding a club.
* Her cult is strongly associated with yellow clothing, yellow substances and the title Pītāmbarā.
* Major contemporary centres include Datia, Nalkheda, Bankhandi/Kangra and a Bagalāmukhī shrine in Patan, Nepal. ([Wikimedia Commons][3])
* Modern devotees explicitly approach her in relation to courts, disputes, hostile speech, politics and victory. This is a living devotional claim, not evidence that supernatural immobilization occurs. ([Navbharat Times][4])
* The Sanskrit National Mission hosts a substantial modern Sanskrit/Hindi study titled *Śrī Bagalāmukhī Tattva Vimarśa*, useful for tracing later textual traditions. ([Central Sanskrit University][5])

## PLAUSIBLE

* The tongue-grasping image dramatizes control over speech rather than merely physical violence.
* *Stambhana* may have operated simultaneously as:

  * a ritual category;
  * a political fantasy of neutralizing opposition;
  * an internal discipline of interrupting impulsive speech;
  * a metaphysical image of arresting manifestation.
* Bagalāmukhī’s intense yellow field may mark ritual specialization and distinct identity rather than a single universal symbol.
* Her later contemplative interpretation as “stilling the mind” plausibly emerges from her older power of stopping outward action, but should not replace the coercive historical layer. The Met explicitly interprets her as checking speech and stilling the mind. ([The Metropolitan Museum of Art][2])

## LATE / HAGIOGRAPHIC / SECTARIAN

* She emerged from a turmeric-coloured lake to halt a cosmic storm.
* Her major temples were established by figures from the *Mahābhārata*.
* A particular temple image is self-manifested.
* A visit guarantees victory in court or the defeat of enemies.
* Datia’s founder possessed an entirely hidden miraculous identity.
* Every use of Bagalāmukhī worship rebounds automatically upon an unethical practitioner.

These claims may be meaningful within living traditions but require separate historical evidence. Official and temple-oriented pages preserve several of them. ([bhupendrguru][6])

## FALSE OR MISLEADING

* “Bagalāmukhī is simply the goddess of black magic.”
* “She literally pulls out the demon’s tongue in every image.”
* “Stambhana only means physical paralysis.”
* “Her cult is ancient Vedic religion unchanged.”
* “Science proves her mantra disables speech.”
* “She is merely a peaceful mindfulness symbol.”
* “Everyone visiting her temple is seeking harm.”
* “The tongue-holding figure represents one securely historical event.”

